Mesh::Clean's spike-removal loop collects spikeVerts in one pass, then mutates the
mesh with Euler::remove_face while iterating that now-stale list. Removing the faces
around one spike can delete a vertex that appears later in the list. Such a vertex
still passes the is_isolated() guard, but its half-edge cycle no longer closes, so
the halfedges_around_target circulator -- which has no termination check -- spins
forever and toRemove grows until the process is OOM-killed.

On a large mesh a single such vertex can push hundreds of thousands of half-edges in
one iteration; unguarded, reconstruct grows to tens of GiB and is SIGKILLed. With the
guard the loop converges in a few iterations at a flat couple of GiB. It fails loudly
every time -- SIGKILL, no output after "Removed N connected components" -- so it
cannot produce a silently bad mesh.

Fixed upstream by rewriting Clean onto the halfmesh library (openMVS #1297 on
develop), which replaces the per-vertex walk with a bulk removal. That rewrite adds
a dependency needing C++20 <format> (GCC 13+; this container has GCC 11.4), so this
is the minimal equivalent guard for the pinned commit.

--- a/libs/MVS/Mesh.cpp
+++ b/libs/MVS/Mesh.cpp
@@ -905,6 +905,12 @@ void Mesh::Clean(float fDecimate, float fSpurious, bool bRemoveSpikes, unsigned
 			if (spikeVerts.empty())
 				break;
 			for (CLEAN::vertex_descriptor vd : spikeVerts) {
+				// spikeVerts was built before any of the removals below ran; an
+				// earlier iteration may already have deleted this vertex, leaving
+				// a half-edge cycle that never closes for the circulator further
+				// down. is_isolated() does not catch that, is_removed() does.
+				if (sm.is_removed(vd))
+					continue;
 				if (sm.is_isolated(vd)) {
 					sm.remove_vertex(vd);
 					continue;
